hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in meditation 3  one of the things that Rene Descartes is  considering that plays an integral role  in the argument and the explanations  that he's providing by which he's  gradually reconstructing his account of  reality is the Providence of our ideas  that is where our ideas are coming from  how are they coming to us are they  within us already are we producing them  are they coming from outside things and  that's the tripart distinction that he  is going to make and then more  importantly rely upon this distinction  is sometimes difficult to follow and to  grasp and it's not helped by the  language that many of the translations  use which which actually comes from  decart's latin which which is good to  take a look at in this case we generally  talk about three types of ideas that  decart himself recognizes and those  three one of them is very important  plays a major role later on doesn't play  such an important role in meditation 3  in 8 and then we have what we call  adventitious ideas right in the Malayan  ex his autem  that's of these ideas ah di and not a  that is some are born with us right Ali  I Advent it ci that's coming from  something else and then we have ideas  that we can call produced and when we  say produced we mean produced by us so  let's let's recap these now actually  looking at dick Hurtz  own words he says of the ideas some seem  to be born with me that's what it means  to be innate that means that these are  ideas that we wouldn't necessarily  already be thinking but we already have  them within us in some way and when we  direct what he calls the natural light  to shine upon them when we're using our  attention we can automatically access  them and they may take a little bit of  you know working with to become  completely clear for us but they're  there they're there already we don't  have to produce them by our own work the  operative powers of our mind they're  already within us just waiting to be  called up then we have ideas that you  know we're calling here adventitious  ideas that are arriving in the french  literally from outside right door and  the notion here is that these are the  ideas that would come to us for example  from sensing visible objects we we see  the object and we get the idea of the  object was the card thinks of it's sort  of a image or a representation of it  uses both of those terms and we have the  idea in our mind that and we could say  that about a smell right the smell is a  certain image of the thing that we're  smelling we smell something because it  comes on us from from the outside or you  know it could be that there really is no  outside and these ideas are coming to us  from some evil demon as he suggested  causing us to lose elucidate in that  case it would still be coming from  something  not coming from us and then he says we  have ideas that are made fit in French  and invented or discovered on Vontae by  myself in in de cartes case he's talking  about himself or by ourselves and you  know what would be some examples of this  well he gives us a number of examples he  tells us I have the Faculty of  conceiving what we in general can call a  thing or a truth or a thought and it  seems that I don't get this from  anything other than my own specific or  proper nature these are ideas that are  so general that I already have them  within my mind I didn't form them by  looking at things in the world but  they're already there for me I use them  to make sense of the things in the world  so to speak when it comes to  adventitious ideas then he says you know  if I hear something a certain sound or  if I see the Sun or if I sense heat up  until this this hour  I've actually judged that these  sentiments are feelings preceded or  arrived from some things that exist  outside of myself and then we can think  of the work of the imagination that  would be a prime example of ideas that I  have created through my own operative  powers so he says you know we can think  of sirens hippogriffs and all the other  similar cámaras which are fictions and  inventions of my own mind so he says  also perhaps I can persuade myself that  all of these ideas would be of that  genre that we call adventitious or  external coming from outside  why because maybe they're they're not  born with me or that they're born with  me or that they were all created by me  because I don't any at this point in  time have a clear idea of their origin  maybe the idea of a unicorn was innate  or maybe I've seen a unicorn somewhere  now  Descartes then says all right what could  be a criterion for telling these things  apart maybe one criterion could be  whether it's up to me whether I can  choose or not choose to produce these  ideas wouldn't that be a great way to  separate them so he says you know with  with some of these reasons you know that  it seems that they're taught to be by  nature and then there's others that I  experience in myself that don't depend  at all on my will often these present  themselves to me despite what I do or  against what I do for you know example  whether I'm you know if I'm awake or  whether I'm not awake I feel heat and  for this cause I am persuaded that the  feeling or rather that idea of heat is  produced in me by something different  than myself or you know we can talk  about all sorts of other things you know  maybe I hear sounds or I smell odors or  I have a taste or I have a feeling and  it's not up to me whether I feel that or  not so he goes on and he says well we've  got to be careful in these distinctions  first off the things that seem to be  innate definitely the things that we see  by the natural light those are innate  and I've discovered some of them already  in the meditations you know for example  that if I doubt I must exist that's  that's something that we can actually  say as a truth is already there within  me right  to be found but I've got these other  things that I call my nature and their  inclinations and sometimes they lead me  astray sometimes they actually don't  take me in the right direction and so  perhaps those are actually produced by  me and then he goes on and considers  something else that's that's quite  interesting he says for the other reason  that these ideas must come from outside  because they don't depend on my volition  or will I actually don't find that  convincing why because just like the  inclinations that I was just talking  about are found in me even though they  don't accord always with my volition or  choice also or similarly perhaps theirs  in me some sort of faculty or power  which is prone to produce these ideas  without the aid or help of any exterior  things even though it doesn't seem to be  known to me so what is he talking about  there he doesn't go into any further  detail we might think about that as  something like the unconscious or you  know our Faculty of putting things  together and in perception against our  own choice when we exercise our  imagination it's not always bringing  things up that we are ourselves choosing  to imagine sometimes our imagination  runs away with us doesn't it but it  would still be ideas that I'm producing  myself they're still coming from me  maybe if it's you know things like  affective states like feelings  associated with a certain thought once I  have that thought the fear or anger or  joy or whatever it is  arises within me well in that case I'm  responsible for producing that now  what's the upshot of this distinction he  you  this continually throughout meditation  three and it's a good one to keep in  mind through the rest of it and he asks  himself where do my ideas of certain  things come from many of them it turns  out he thinks could not be adventitious  but could be produced maybe there is no  external world and maybe I'm the one  who's actually producing most of these  ideas whether I realize it or not so he  talks about a number of different ideas  of things that we have at our disposal  at this point what is the idea of myself  and he says that one you know I've  already got I've already thought about  myself it includes understanding and  willing and denying and affirming and  loving and hating and it goes on and on  with all the different modes of thought  but there's something else that's  important in this as well in  understanding his idea of himself he has  grasped himself as a substance as a  thing that thinks right he's also  grasped himself in terms of duration and  in terms of number and this is going to  be quite important when it comes to  thinking about other corporeal things  that are non animate he says that I  could have gotten my idea of these other  corporeal things I could be producing  themselves by taking these these basic  components of the idea of myself and  then just you know changing them around  in different ways whether I realize I'm  doing it whether I'm consciously doing  it or whether I'm just sort of happening  you know to do this in the background  and then I discover these ideas of books  and the world and air and all these  other sorts of things so maybe the idea  of corporeal things is really something  instead of being adventitious instead of  coming from outside things that those  ideas would be representations of maybe  I've produced those what about other  things like people or  animals or angels Descartes says I could  have produced those ideas notice that he  doesn't say I could have produced them  by thinking about myself and then  projecting another self out there he  says I could have produced those by  taking the idea of God and corporeal  things and combining them in some way so  as to get beings that would be animate  that would have agency that would have  certain perfections I so to speak place  them in between God at the one extreme  and inanimate corporeal things at the  other and put them like right here in  the middle as existing things that have  a kind of agency sort of like God what  about my idea of God this is going to be  the one idea Descartes says that I  definitely could not have produced so  his you know long story short it's  actually going to turn out to be in an  eighth idea rather than an adventitious  idea although in a certain sense you  could say as an innate idea it is an  adventitious idea because it's so to  speak the stamp that the creator has  placed within a certain kind of creation  right human being but the upshot here is  that the idea of God as opposed to these  other ideas which either I derive for  myself because it is the idea of myself  or I derive for myself by taking ideas  that I've already got at my disposal and  combining them and recombining them and  modifying them in certain ways the idea  of God cannot be something like that for  take heart that's going to be a linchpin  for the argument here and that's part of  why this distinction between different  main classes of ideas based on their  origins is going to be so central in  meditation 3  you
 

